.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt MEMFD_CREATE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm memfd_create
.Nd make a file in memory that no path names
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd "#define _GNU_SOURCE"
.In sys/mman.h
.Ft int
.Fn memfd_create "const char *name" "unsigned int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn memfd_create
makes an empty regular file in memory and returns a new descriptor open
on it for reading and writing, at offset 0.
The file is on a tmpfs the kernel keeps for these files, which no path
reaches and no listing shows; it goes when the last descriptor, mapping
or description of it goes.
It reads, writes, seeks, truncates and maps as a tmpfs file does.
Its owner is the caller's effective user and group, and its mode 0777,
not masked by the umask.
.Pp
.Fa name
is at most 249 bytes and is for people:
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
shows the descriptor as
.Ql /memfd: Ns Ar name Ns Ql " (deleted)" ,
as Linux does, and nothing else reads it.
.Pp
.Fa flags
is a combination of:
.Bl -tag -width MFD_ALLOW_SEALING
.It Dv MFD_CLOEXEC
Set
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
on the descriptor.
.It Dv MFD_ALLOW_SEALING
Make the file without seals, so that
.Xr fcntl 2 Ns 's
.Dv F_ADD_SEALS
can add them.
Without it the file has
.Dv F_SEAL_SEAL ,
which refuses every other seal.
.It Dv MFD_NOEXEC_SEAL
(8) Make the file with mode 0666 and the seal
.Dv F_SEAL_EXEC ,
and allow sealing, as Linux 6.3 does.
.It Dv MFD_EXEC
(0x10) Make the file with mode 0777, which it has anyway.
.El
.Ss Seals
.Xr fcntl 2 Ns 's
.Dv F_GET_SEALS
reports the file's seals and
.Dv F_ADD_SEALS
adds to them; they are never removed.
.Bl -tag -width F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE
.It Dv F_SEAL_SEAL
Refuse any seal more.
.It Dv F_SEAL_SHRINK
Refuse a
.Xr truncate 2
or
.Xr ftruncate 2
to a smaller size.
.It Dv F_SEAL_GROW
Refuse a truncation to a larger size and a write that would pass the
end; such a write fails whole, with nothing written, and so does a
.Xr writev 2
whose elements together would pass the end.
.It Dv F_SEAL_WRITE , F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE
Refuse a
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr pwrite64 2
and
.Xr writev 2 .
The kernel does not refuse a shared mapping of a sealed file that may
write, and a mapping made before the seal keeps writing through it;
Linux refuses a new one and, for
.Dv F_SEAL_WRITE ,
refuses the seal with
.Er EBUSY
while one exists.
.It Dv F_SEAL_EXEC
Refuse a change of the execute bits by
.Xr chmod 2 ;
a file that has execute bits when it is sealed so is sealed against
writing too.
.El
.Pp
Every tmpfs file supports the seal commands, and one made by
.Xr open 2
starts with
.Dv F_SEAL_SEAL ;
a file of any other type answers them with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The i386 system call number is 356.
The kernel takes the name in EBX and the flags in ECX.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn memfd_create
returns the new descriptor, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/memfdmusl.c
makes a memfd that allows sealing, checks its descriptor, its mode, its
size and its name under
.Pa /proc/self/fd ,
then adds each seal in turn and checks the writes, a
.Xr writev 2
among them, and the truncations it refuses, and makes one without
.Dv MFD_ALLOW_SEALING
and one with
.Dv MFD_NOEXEC_SEAL .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EMFILE]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa name
cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa name
is longer than 249 bytes,
.Fa flags
has
.Dv MFD_HUGETLB
or a bit that is no flag, or both
.Dv MFD_NOEXEC_SEAL
and
.Dv MFD_EXEC .
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The process has no free descriptor below its limit.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel heap has no room for the file, a larger descriptor table or
an open-file description.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr truncate 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
